Sofia's posture shifts slightly, as if debating whether to speak. After a moment, she exhales and meets your gaze.
<b>I see you found my cabin.</b> Her tone remains composed, but there’s a flicker of hesitation in her eyes. <b>Yes, it’s mine. A leader doesn’t get much peace in a place like this. Even I need somewhere to clear my head.</b> 
She crosses her arms. <b>But I wasn’t the only one there that night.</b>
She hesitates, then exhales. <b>Sheila came to see me. She wanted to talk in private, away from town. She seemed… tense. She didn’t say why, not at first.</b> 
Sofia looks away for a moment, gathering her thoughts. <b>I left before midnight. She was alive when I walked away. That’s the truth.</b> Her voice hardens slightly, her grip tightening on her sleeve.
<b>I should’ve stayed. I should’ve made her come back with me.</b> Her jaw clenches, but she forces herself to stay composed. <b> But regretting the past won’t help us now. Focus on what’s in front of us.</b>
